    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 182249\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n349 PM PDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Clearing skies and cool conditions for most areas today but non-\n  impactful rain is possible again Monday morning for Mohave Co.\n  \n* A stronger system will bring increased precipitation chances and \n  gusty winds to the region the middle to end of the week.  \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next weekend.\n\nThe lingering showers from the early morning are eroding along with \nmost of the cloud cover, leading to clearing skies for the mid to \nlate afternoon and mostly clear skies overnight. This will allow for \nslightly warmer afternoon temperatures with highs in the upper 70s \nand low 80s for most of the region (Death Valley will still peak in \nthe low 90s). The clearing skies will allow for good cooling \novernight with most lows falling into the 60s (70s for Death \nValley). Cloud cover returns to the western portions of the CWA into \nafternoon Monday with a few more non-impactful showers possible. \nThese rain chances look to mostly stick to Mohave Co and dissipate \ninto the night.\n\nThe next rain system will not be as meek. A trough approaches the \narea into the mid week with initial showers and light snow chances \n(for the highest peaks) arriving at the Sierra Nevada early Tuesday. \nChances for rain on this initial push look good, but there wont be \nmuch in the way of rainfall mounts until the trough digs deeper into \nsouthern California and pulls more moisture from the Pacific on \nWednesday. The orientation of that moisture and the trough itself \nwill offer widespread chances for rain Wednesday and Thursday before \nexiting on Friday. Models continue to entertain some snow \naccumulations above 9000 ft, but the moisture fails to line up with \nthe colder air behind the trough, limiting snow amounts. Rain for \nthe lower elevations looks light to moderate for most of Wednesday \nwith a wave of moderate to locally increased rain rates possible \nlate Wednesday into Thursday morning. Rainfall accumulations look \nfairly manageable with most areas seeing 0.10\" to 0.50\" and higher \npeaks approaching 0.75\" by the time the rain exits on early Friday. \nWinds will be more of a factor Wednesday afternoon with 40 mph gusts \npossible, but easing that night and not really returning for \nThursday. Temperatures will cool into the weekend with highs peaking \naround 70 for most areas, and Death Valley peaking in the low 80s. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds \nfollow typical daily directional patterns with speeds under 8 knots. \nMid and high level clouds will gradually decrease in coverage this \nafternoon and evening with bases at or above 10kft. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds generally follow \ntypical daily directional patterns with some periods of variability. \nSpeeds remain under 8 knots for most locations. At KBIH, southerly \nwinds increase to around 10 knots with moderate gusts tomorrow \nafternoon. Mid and high cloud cover will decrease and move east \ntoday and tonight with bases at or above 10kft. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
